# Go service environment — copy to .env.local for local development.
#
#   cp api/.env.example api/.env.local
#
# .env.local is gitignored. This template is not, and must never contain real values.
#
# In production these are Fly secrets (`fly secrets set`), never committed to fly.toml.
# Full reference: docs/06-deployment.md
#
# internal/config/config.go (T-05) loads these and fails fast, listing every missing
# required variable at once. DATABASE_URL becomes required in T-06; the JWT variables
# become required in T-08.

# --- Database ----------------------------------------------------------------
# SECRET. Supabase connection string, POOLER variant on port 6543 — not the direct
# connection on 5432. Supabase caps direct connections tightly and a pgx pool will
# exhaust them. Dashboard: Project Settings -> Database -> Connection string -> Pooler.
#
# postgresql://postgres.<ref>:<password>@aws-0-<region>.pooler.supabase.com:6543/postgres
DATABASE_URL=

# --- Auth (see docs/04-auth.md) ----------------------------------------------
# The JWKS endpoint for the project's public signing keys. Not a secret.
# Used only when the project signs JWTs with ASYMMETRIC keys, which is the path
# docs/01-architecture.md assumes and T-01 should confirm.
SUPABASE_JWKS_URL=https://<project-ref>.supabase.co/auth/v1/.well-known/jwks.json

# Checked against the token's `iss` claim.
SUPABASE_JWT_ISS=https://<project-ref>.supabase.co/auth/v1

# Checked against the token's `aud` claim.
SUPABASE_JWT_AUD=authenticated

# LEGACY SHARED-SECRET MODE ONLY — leave unset on the asymmetric path.
# If T-01 finds the project signs with a shared HS256 secret, T-08 implements that
# path instead: uncomment this, drop SUPABASE_JWKS_URL, and allow HS256 only.
# Implement ONE path, never both — a dead branch is where a verification bug hides.
# SUPABASE_JWT_SECRET=

# --- Server ------------------------------------------------------------------
# Fly sets PORT at runtime; config reads it rather than hardcoding.
PORT=8080

# development | production
# development enables CORS for the Expo web dev server on :8081 and human-readable
# logs. production emits JSON logs and suppresses error detail in responses.
ENV=development
